WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.146 --> 00:00:15.800
We have Alan Kay with us this week,
he's going to do both lectures.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.860
Alan Kay has forgotten more about how
to invent the future than progressives

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.906
&gt;&gt; That would make a very bad class, Sam,

00:00:23.906 --> 00:00:24.472
if I have.

00:00:24.472 --> 00:00:27.166
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I think Alan is the genuine

00:00:27.166 --> 00:00:28.685
world expert on this.

00:00:28.685 --> 00:00:33.657
Invented with others, the Xerox Alto,
which we recently restored at

00:00:33.657 --> 00:00:38.390
Y Combinator with a group of
incredible computer scientists.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.550
And I think it's the person who has been
most thoughtful, probably with anyone I've

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.740
ever met, about how you build
organizations to do real innovations.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.120
So thank you very much for coming,
I am super excited to hear this.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.360
&gt;&gt; Great, Sam.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.110
So, I'm thoughtful about it because
I got to watch masters do it.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.660
I'm basically a research scientist, but

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.000
I was interested in the process done
by far better managers than me,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.770
who could deal with larger sets of ideas.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.750
And I'll try and give you a gist here.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.809
Basically, I think,

00:01:13.809 --> 00:01:18.690
people are here because they
want to do startups and make money.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.532
I just want to point out that
if you want to make money,

00:01:21.532 --> 00:01:23.820
don't bother with a startup.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.839
Create an industry, because then you
get trillions instead of billions.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:36.530
So it's about a factor of a thousand
between doing invention over innovation.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.170
In other words, not going
incrementally from the present but

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.516
carving out a whole new set of ideas
that creates an entirely new context.

00:01:44.516 --> 00:01:48.223
And we'll see some of
the ways to go about it and

00:01:48.223 --> 00:01:51.278
we'll also see some of the barriers.

00:01:51.278 --> 00:01:54.262
And see if this, no.

00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:56.350
This got a bad user interface,

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:00.138
because it has the back button
near to the front button.

00:02:00.138 --> 00:02:02.820
See if that works, so.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.890
A lot of what these two
days are about are talking

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.260
about the place that most people
naturally live, which is the present.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.470
But the problem with the present, it is so
glittery and distracting, there's so

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.560
much stuff going on.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.000
That it's hard to think about
anything except the present.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.140
And if you're thinking about the present,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.480
your ideas are going to be
derived from the present.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.130
And therefore,
they're going to be incremental.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:30.330
And therefore,
you wind up doing innovation,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.451
not that you can't make money that way,
but.

00:02:33.451 --> 00:02:37.964
Today, I'm going to do
the opposite of my usual order,

00:02:37.964 --> 00:02:41.890
I usually like to build
a lot of context first.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:46.708
But I thought in the spirit of the Harvard
Business School and the equivalent at

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:51.819
Stanford, I'd start off with things like
results and process and methods first.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:56.082
So you have a feeling that I'm actually
[COUGH] saying something that you might

00:02:56.082 --> 00:02:57.338
quote on quote apply.

00:02:57.338 --> 00:03:00.796
But the important talk
is really on Thursday,

00:03:00.796 --> 00:03:04.794
I'm still struggling with
making it the right size.

00:03:04.794 --> 00:03:09.832
So today we're going to do that, and
I urge you to copy my email address.

00:03:09.832 --> 00:03:14.373
A lot of you emails,
we're not going to have time

00:03:14.373 --> 00:03:18.910
to have the discussion
we should have here.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.258
I welcome any questions and
by the way, while I'm talking,

00:03:22.258 --> 00:03:24.961
I welcome questions at any time,
don't be shy.

00:03:27.891 --> 00:03:31.970
Okay and here's why school pisses me off.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.224
A great man in our field, Marvin Minsky
observed that this is the best place ever

00:03:38.224 --> 00:03:41.639
to keep you from ever thinking
about anything for long enough.

00:03:44.018 --> 00:03:47.360
So you don't want to try and
learn in a classroom, it's terrible.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.114
In fact,
I don't like to do classroom process,

00:03:51.114 --> 00:03:54.330
partly because of the time constraints.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.410
But also because it's oral,

00:03:56.410 --> 00:04:00.730
we might as well be sitting around
a campfire, a hundred thousand years ago.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.350
And almost everything that's happened
that's important in the last

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.430
several thousand of years,
has been basically, literary informed.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.650
Okay, so
one of my favorite Picasso sayings,

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.940
and he meant a lot of
different things by this.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.550
Part of it is the best you can ever do
with any kind of representation is make

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:27.270
a kind of a map, even when you're
trying to make a map of a map.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.350
You're making a lie compared to
the thing you're trying to represent.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.230
But if you do it right,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:38.580
people can gain some intuition
about what the map is about.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.205
That's what science is all about.

00:04:40.205 --> 00:04:47.310
This talk in 50 minutes has to be a lie,
it really is.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:52.915
I'm leaving out a lot of important things.

00:04:52.915 --> 00:04:57.378
But I think the shadow of the talk or
whatever is projected by the talk is

00:04:57.378 --> 00:05:02.690
pretty close to the way things actually
are for doing this very different process.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.380
And I think Picasso didn't say,
but he knew and

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:13.160
he meant, is art is also the lie that
tells the truth that wakes you up.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.475
So if something wakes you up in the next
two days, I will have done my job.

00:05:19.475 --> 00:05:24.904
And then, the two days are really
about another Picasso quote which is,

00:05:24.904 --> 00:05:29.540
learn the rules like a pro so
you can break them like an artist.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:34.160
And not doing this is probably
the greatest sin of Silicon Valley

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:34.990
since the 80s.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:42.540
Almost never, nobody has bothered becoming
a pro particularly in software and

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:43.560
user interface design.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:46.729
So when they break the rules,

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:50.164
they're breaking them like a dumb child
does by throwing rocks through windows.

00:05:50.164 --> 00:05:54.540
And it's probably the most sickening
thing to see about the field.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.520
So another way of thinking
about the mantra is,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.630
you have to learn everything and
then find a way of forgetting it.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:05.140
So you can have your own ideas, but

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:09.380
what you forget is everything
except the perfume.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.928
So when you have an idea then your
nose will pick up the right scent.

00:06:13.928 --> 00:06:17.755
And you'll be able to make use of all of
the stuff that you've learned after you've

00:06:17.755 --> 00:06:18.419
had the idea.

00:06:24.347 --> 00:06:27.550
I was specifically asked
to talk about Xerox PARC

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:32.170
because that was an example of making
trillions instead of billions.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:37.491
And, so
I thought what I'll do here is just show

00:06:37.491 --> 00:06:42.289
you a couple of results, from Xerox PARC.

00:06:42.289 --> 00:06:47.584
And I'll then try to give you some of
what it took to get those results.

00:06:47.584 --> 00:06:54.230
So, Xerox PARC is known for this machine
that happened, I gave one to Sam.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:59.740
He got it working with the help
of some really great people.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:03.501
So this machine happened in 1973
which is 11 years before the Mac, and

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:05.984
its screen was more than
twice the size of the Mac.

00:07:05.984 --> 00:07:10.590
It was more like a Mac of 1988 or 1989.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:15.890
So it was about maybe 15 or 16 years
ahead of the commercial development.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:17.350
And the commercial development,

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:22.680
actually, was based on the stuff
that was done on this machine.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.627
So this is an example of the best way
to predict the future is to invent it.

00:07:25.627 --> 00:07:29.660
There's nothing like this before.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.019
Once we did it, people could see, yeah,

00:07:32.019 --> 00:07:35.780
there could be something like
this because here it is.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.550
In fact, we made 2000 of these,
so there's a lot to look at.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.361
They have bit map screens,
they had pointing device.

00:07:41.361 --> 00:07:45.980
The famous GUI,
which you're still using today.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:52.010
WYSIWYG means what you
see is what you get.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:54.170
Desktop publishing and
the whole media gig.

00:07:57.908 --> 00:07:58.780
What does that say?

00:08:00.699 --> 00:08:03.907
That's not helping me, anybody read that?

00:08:03.907 --> 00:08:05.762
&gt;&gt; Symmetrical reading and writing.

00:08:05.762 --> 00:08:07.943
&gt;&gt; Yeah, symmetrical reading and writing.

00:08:07.943 --> 00:08:12.984
Meaning what you almost never get
on the web, but that you did get in

00:08:12.984 --> 00:08:18.116
many of the apps of the 1980s,
which is when you get a document,

00:08:18.116 --> 00:08:24.080
the thing that you read the document with
also allows you to edit the document.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:28.727
So if you think about the web,
it's actually much more made for

00:08:28.727 --> 00:08:32.631
consumption than it is made for
authoring, they're completely decoupled.

00:08:32.631 --> 00:08:36.855
And most of the authoring facilities
are typing in tiny little windows, and

00:08:36.855 --> 00:08:39.170
then pushing a button to see what you did.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:42.750
So this is a vast retrogression.

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:45.870
What we call real loop these days,

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.470
we just called it object
oriented programming back then.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.510
I made up that term.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.280
But what's called object
oriented programming today is

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.750
not what we had back then.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:58.800
And I don't think I'll have time to really
talk about the profound difference.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:04.440
So if I have to say that what we
did at PARC became popular, and

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.050
everybody wanted it even
if they only got the label.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:14.670
Comparison is you can go out and buy a set
of designer jeans with the label Harvard

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:19.806
on them, and for
all I know with Stanford on them.

00:09:19.806 --> 00:09:24.205
Laser printer,
main difference between this and

00:09:24.205 --> 00:09:29.030
what you have today is the first
one was a page a second.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.900
So most people have never printed
with a page a second printer.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:37.160
Postscript, outline fonts and so forth.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:39.230
Ethernet.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:45.810
Peer-peer and client server,
and 50% of the Internet.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.205
PARC did an Internet before
there was the Internet.

00:09:48.205 --> 00:09:53.740
And we're part of that community, so
we participated in the official Internet.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.160
So we think of that as eight and
a half inventions.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:58.200
How long did it take?

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.060
Well, 25 researchers for five years.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:02.580
Think about that.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:07.444
Two dozen people did all of these
things in about five years.

00:10:07.444 --> 00:10:12.220
Cost about 10 million to 12 million
bucks a year in today's money.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:16.780
Return is about 35 trillion,
this is an old number,

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.630
it's probably more like 40 now.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:23.230
So that's pretty good return on
investment if you think about it.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:25.375
If you like to do little spreadsheets and
stuff.

00:10:25.375 --> 00:10:29.060
15% is good, right Sam?

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:36.670
Yeah, so, and it was an industry
rather than an increment.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:41.238
People say, well, but
Xerox didn't benefit from this.

00:10:41.238 --> 00:10:43.270
They didn't make any money for
it, that's complete ****.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:47.100
It's made up by companies that
don't want to invest in research.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.950
Xerox made about a factor
of 250 over their entire

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:55.700
investment in PARC, so
that's 25,000% return right there.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:59.821
Xerox's bug was they didn't understand
the rest of this stuff, but they

00:10:59.821 --> 00:11:04.357
certainly understood what a laser printer
was, and they made billions from it.

00:11:04.357 --> 00:11:08.662
And in fact, if you look at it,
it was better for

00:11:08.662 --> 00:11:12.001
the world that they stonewalled us,

00:11:12.001 --> 00:11:17.293
because no single company can
handle an entire industry.

00:11:19.222 --> 00:11:23.223
So this is made it possible: the Japanese
had to do printers slightly,

00:11:23.223 --> 00:11:24.652
differently than that.

00:11:24.652 --> 00:11:32.090
And this, kind of heaven on Earth,
lasted for about 12 years,

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:39.398
Xerox finally fired the guy
who made it all happen.

00:11:39.398 --> 00:11:43.310
You're thinking,
he would have been rewarded,

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:48.180
right, because he made all this stuff
happen, but in fact, they hated him.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.220
And they hated him for

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:53.210
the very reason that most companies hate
people who are doing something different,

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:57.269
because it makes middle management and
upper management extremely uncomfortable.

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.580
Last thing they want to
do is make trillions.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:03.460
What they want to do is make a few
millions in a comfortable way.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:09.800
And so this leads to
an enormous problem that where

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:15.490
the real job of upper management in the 20
and 21st century is to learn things,

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.748
because change is the constant
thing that's going on.

00:12:20.748 --> 00:12:25.550
What they tried to do is to maximize
whatever they had when they got

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:28.010
into the job, and
whatever the company was successful at.

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:31.531
If you think about it,
if large companies were actually rational,

00:12:31.531 --> 00:12:33.395
there'd never be a small company.

00:12:33.395 --> 00:12:39.234
There'd be no startups, because large
companies have vastly more resources for

00:12:39.234 --> 00:12:43.476
doing new things than any
venture capitalist, right Sam?

00:12:43.476 --> 00:12:48.695
They've got gazillions of money,
but because they refuse to get into

00:12:48.695 --> 00:12:54.900
new businesses and refuse to change their
old businesses, you guys have a chance.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.690
So, just pray that they
don't ever wake up.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:05.170
Now, the interesting thing is if you
look at that collection of inventions,

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:06.980
you see every single one of them today.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:13.370
And what's interesting is if I were to
enlarge this a bit, I would show that

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:18.720
there's hardly been anything interesting
invented since this funding stopped.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:24.470
So it's been a nuke,
basically a nuclear winter of

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:32.270
people cashing in on these inventions,
and extracting the wealth from them, but

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:36.220
hardly, any efforts of any
interesting kind to go beyond them.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.917
And last point here is
the lack of curiosity.

00:13:41.917 --> 00:13:48.970
I met Sam, because he wanted to know
where'd all this stuff come from?

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:50.750
I've hardly ever been asked that question.

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:57.480
It's the most successful generation
of wealth in computing history,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:01.527
and almost nobody wants to know, Sam did.

00:14:05.229 --> 00:14:11.390
And the difficult thing about today and
Thursday is the world that you grew up in,

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:15.928
because I don't see anybody,
is anybody here older than 35?

00:14:15.928 --> 00:14:19.082
Okay, yeah, I see a little
more reflectivity back there.

00:14:19.082 --> 00:14:22.398
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:14:22.398 --> 00:14:26.647
almost everybody in the room grew up in
a world that was unlike the world I'm

00:14:26.647 --> 00:14:28.410
going to just tell you about now.

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:33.571
So it's just many, many things were
qualitatively different in ways that

00:14:33.571 --> 00:14:35.682
are sometimes difficult to explain.

00:14:38.732 --> 00:14:40.150
So here's the book to read.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:43.062
This is why lectures suck.

00:14:43.062 --> 00:14:47.556
This is a 500 page book
done very carefully

00:14:47.556 --> 00:14:52.070
about the whole story of ARPA and
Xerox PARC.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:54.874
Anybody who's really interested,
read this book.

00:14:54.874 --> 00:14:59.944
And this is a tribute I
wrote to this whole research

00:14:59.944 --> 00:15:05.390
community about I don't know 14,
15 years ago.

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:08.790
And I'll hand it out at
the end of the class, and

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:14.060
the main thing that is interesting
about it perhaps is the bibliography,

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:18.650
which has a lot of references on
more detailed things you can read

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:22.731
about how this particular
community operated, right?

00:15:22.731 --> 00:15:26.891
And the important thing about Xerox PARC,
which is not emphasized enough,

00:15:26.891 --> 00:15:30.407
is that PARC was just another one
of the ARPA research projects.

00:15:32.856 --> 00:15:40.270
So there's eight years of
research before PARC happened.

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:42.570
And PARC only happened
because of the Vietnam War.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:46.600
Nobody wanted to try to do this
stuff inside of a company.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:48.940
A company quarterly cycles and

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:53.790
everything else are really
antithetical to long range thinking.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:57.760
So this stuff was all funded by
Cold War funds in the public domain.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:04.690
So none of the IP was kept secret,
but it was done in a better rhythm.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:10.740
So to get the 35 trillion,
it actually required not five years,

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:11.406
but 12 to 14 years.

00:16:11.406 --> 00:16:18.104
We were lucky researchers who
got our PhDs in this process and

00:16:18.104 --> 00:16:23.764
we're the right age to go to park and
finish it off.

00:16:25.977 --> 00:16:31.143
Right and as we'll see I'm not
going to dwell on it much more but

00:16:31.143 --> 00:16:35.194
the circa of this way of
doing things goes back,

00:16:35.194 --> 00:16:38.639
especially to the radar effort at MIT and

00:16:38.639 --> 00:16:43.020
the air defense effort and
then upper and the park.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:49.184
So there is a long continuity
here of kind of how do you,

00:16:49.184 --> 00:16:56.609
work on things that are,
That are doable but where you have to

00:16:56.609 --> 00:17:01.440
invent several generations of technology
to get to the thing that's doable.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:06.440
So most of these things are not doable
with the technology that's lying around.

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:10.050
That is the problem with
these hard problems.

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:14.450
Okay, so the general world,
the normal world.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.050
The present, for most of this period,

