00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:53.473
The utilities, so he thought there
would be an information utility, and

00:25:53.473 --> 00:25:59.530
it will actually be a human right,
like the telephone, to have

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:04.750
one of these things In your home that is
connected to all the worlds information.

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:08.315
So that was one of the earliest and
most influential ideas.

00:26:11.128 --> 00:26:17.730
1962, I'm going to show you a system
done on one of these super computers.

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:23.270
This is done on the test computer at
Lincoln Labs for this whole SAGE system so

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:29.100
this computer was close to
the size of this entire building.

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:33.680
With one guy on it,
yeah at 3 o'clock in the morning.

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:35.600
So, take a look at this.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:39.270
This is Ivan Sutherland.

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:44.850
So, it doesn't really even have a display.

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:47.070
It's actually simulating
a computer display here.

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:48.300
This is just an oscilloscope.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:55.480
So, what Ivan wants to do,
is to draw a flange and so he says,

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:57.500
it's kind of like this.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:01.830
Now take these guys and
make them all mutually perpendicular and

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:05.250
wow, sketch pad just solved that problem.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:09.330
So it's a dynamic problem solver in there.

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:11.030
First window then clipped.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.847
Now it wants to put a hole in the flange.

00:27:15.847 --> 00:27:18.000
So these are guidelines.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.390
and the first thing he does is said,
okay, I want to make this parallel.

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:25.360
And you see, sketch pad keeps them
on the line there and lines them up.

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:29.883
And now, he's using them as
guidelines to draw a dash lines.

00:27:38.086 --> 00:27:41.103
You see here, he misses Whoop, okay.

00:27:43.484 --> 00:27:46.180
Okay, and
now he makes the guidelines invisible.

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:46.992
They're still there.

00:27:48.779 --> 00:27:52.169
The constraint was co-linearity there.

00:27:54.218 --> 00:27:56.550
And he has a knob to continuously zoom.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:28:00.650
So this is the first
computer graphics ever.

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:07.260
And he wants some rivets to go along
with that flange, and so again he,

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:11.460
this is why this system is called a sketch
pad because you just casually draw.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:15.989
Just going to use that as the center for
the ark here.

00:28:19.708 --> 00:28:22.188
And again he's going to say,
take these guys and

00:28:22.188 --> 00:28:26.720
make them mutually perpendicular and
here sketch pad solves that problem.

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:28.408
So you wind up with a symmetric object.

00:28:28.408 --> 00:28:34.712
And he can change things and

00:28:34.712 --> 00:28:41.875
he'll get another solution.

00:28:41.875 --> 00:28:48.230
He could have constrained the side
links to be ratios of each other.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.884
And the kind of problem
solving this system could do,

00:28:53.884 --> 00:28:56.253
well included non linear problems.

00:28:56.253 --> 00:28:58.030
So there, he's got a rivet.

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:02.286
And what's cool about this,
that is a master rivet,

00:29:02.286 --> 00:29:04.970
what we'd call a class in
object oriented programming.

00:29:07.246 --> 00:29:12.555
So this is not that rivet, but
an instance of that master rivet.

00:29:16.529 --> 00:29:18.729
Might wonder, why is that display
bouncing around like that.

00:29:18.729 --> 00:29:21.812
&gt;&gt; Well it's putting up
every dot individually,

00:29:21.812 --> 00:29:26.680
at about half the power of this super
computer is being used to just do that.

00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:30.125
So here another instance, another rivet.

00:29:30.125 --> 00:29:32.332
Here is another one, here's another one.

00:29:34.548 --> 00:29:38.748
And he says whoops I didn't want
to have those crossbars there, so

00:29:38.748 --> 00:29:43.030
I'll go back to the master rivet and
make the crossbars invisible.

00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:46.250
They're still there but invisible,
and we see the instances.

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:48.540
So this is object oriented programming.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:49.950
Maybe nicer than you've seen it.

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:55.472
Get rid of those guys and now,

00:29:57.488 --> 00:30:02.140
that construction that he made,
he's made it into a master.

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:04.323
So he can make instances of it.

00:30:07.109 --> 00:30:08.318
Okay, get the idea?

00:30:08.318 --> 00:30:10.198
&gt;&gt; I had a question.

00:30:10.198 --> 00:30:13.770
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Sort of regressed

