SketchPad before?

00:30:22.816 --> 00:30:26.029
Okay, so that's part of the answer.

00:30:28.430 --> 00:30:33.900
Yeah we'll go along, it's sort of
the larger question that we have.

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:37.662
It's worthwhile bringing up the question
He's rotating three of them.

00:30:41.598 --> 00:30:44.725
So, this is the most shocking thing
I;d ever seen when I went to graduate

00:30:44.725 --> 00:30:45.460
school in 66.

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:53.330
This was, the system was only
three years old at that time.

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:56.340
And I've been programming for
five years and just for

00:30:56.340 --> 00:31:01.110
conventional programming and
seeing this, it blew my mind.

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:05.970
Because it was exactly different than
everything I thought about computing.

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:10.080
As soon as I saw I, I realized yeah,
of course you can do that.

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:13.599
But I didn't think about that.

00:31:13.599 --> 00:31:15.007
Ivan did.

00:31:20.154 --> 00:31:27.700
Okay so Sketchpad, interactive computer
graphics in a way we recognize today.

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:31.340
For the first time, objects,
masters and instances.

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:35.590
The programming was not done by
the kind of programming today, but

00:31:35.590 --> 00:31:38.509
by problem solving, which I think
you can imagine is much nicer.

00:31:39.940 --> 00:31:41.280
Like it will find the solution.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:47.080
And it creates automatic
dynamics simulations,

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:49.170
so it's not just for drawing bridges.

00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:52.480
When you draw the bridge in,
it knows how to simulate the bridge and

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:54.950
tell you what the stresses
in this trains are.

00:31:56.090 --> 00:32:01.215
So I said to Ivan, Ivan you did all
of this and what is this PhD thesis?

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:05.207
Said, you did all this in one year by
yourself, how could you possibly do it?

00:32:05.207 --> 00:32:07.644
And he said, well,
I didn't know it was hard.

00:32:07.644 --> 00:32:11.190
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He just went after what the problem is.

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:12.830
And if you read his thesis,
and you should,

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:18.280
because you can see there are things that
are of absolute goodness still today.

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:22.260
It's not just relatively good for
being done more than 50 years,

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:23.200
it's absolutely good.

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:28.795
And his thesis is, every other page is
an apology because it doesn't do more.

00:32:28.795 --> 00:32:30.882
because Ivan was working on the problem.

00:32:30.882 --> 00:32:34.680
He wasn't working out what you could do,
he was working on the problem.

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:40.570
Yeah, so sketch pad was a bombshell in

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:42.575
this research community
that was about to happen.

00:32:42.575 --> 00:32:46.130
because right away,
