And that allowed Licklider to fund 15 or
20 super

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:48.860
smart people who thought they had
ways of approaching the dream.

00:34:48.860 --> 00:34:53.312
And some of these people didn't
[BLANK AUDIO] agree, and

00:34:53.312 --> 00:34:55.591
some of them hated each other.

00:34:55.591 --> 00:35:00.760
And Lick didn't give a ****, he just
wanted smart people working on this dream.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:04.920
So fund people, not projects.

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:07.470
ARPA never decided.

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:11.360
And we'll see a slight
modification of this.

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:15.190
But basically, fund people, not projects.

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:18.520
If you're going to do that,
you better have the very best people.

00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:23.120
So, if you know,
MacArthur grants are for individuals.

00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:26.750
But this funding was funding groups.

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:29.690
Like you fund in MacArthur,
just five years, forget it.

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:35.690
Nobody at MacArthur asks,
you don't get a second MacArthur grant.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:39.550
Here, they would get another grant if
they'd done something good in five years,

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:42.790
but basically the idea of
MacArthur is throw away.

00:35:44.380 --> 00:35:48.700
We've identified this person of extreme
potential, let's just give him five years

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:51.528
of funding and
we won't cry if they don't do anything.

00:35:51.528 --> 00:35:56.268
It turns out most MacArthur people
do something because the people that

00:35:56.268 --> 00:36:00.299
attract attention are people
who are not working for money.

00:36:01.330 --> 00:36:02.815
They're doing it because they must,

00:36:02.815 --> 00:36:07.280
they are people like artists are people
who do their art because they must.

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:16.230
Community not a project,
have to fund problem finding,

00:36:16.230 --> 00:36:18.010
this really drives funders crazy today.

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:21.890
What do I mean by that?

00:36:21.890 --> 00:36:23.600
Most of the time,
when you are working on hard problems,

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:25.360
you don't know what the right problem is.

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:26.840
This is why visions are really good.

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:29.330
Visions are detached.

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:34.410
If you pick a problem too early you might
be picking it out of the current context.

00:36:35.670 --> 00:36:39.400
And therefore, you're going to be
hampered unless you are incredibly lucky.

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:43.330
So ARPA put a lot of money into just
people thinking around with stuff.

00:36:44.840 --> 00:36:46.350
Milestones not deadlines.

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:49.050
Baseball not golf.

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:54.510
Meaning, when you lose a stroke in golf,
you cry.

00:36:55.910 --> 00:37:00.170
When, you strike out in baseball, you
better not cry, because you're going to do

00:37:00.170 --> 00:37:05.630
it a lot, and
this is what Licklider said to them.

00:37:05.630 --> 00:37:10.180
He said look, if you're batting 3.50
in baseball, you're really doing well.

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:14.141
And if you look at what we're
funding if we bat 3.50,

00:37:14.141 --> 00:37:15.677
we're going to change the world.

00:37:15.677 --> 00:37:16.477
That's what happened.

00:37:16.477 --> 00:37:21.040
Nobody cares about all
the stuff that didn't work.

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:24.544
And people said,
well what about the 65% failure?

00:37:24.544 --> 00:37:29.130
And they said, well it's not
failure in baseball, it's overhead.

00:37:30.790 --> 00:37:35.300
Hitting a ball is hard, when you're doing
something really, really hard, the times

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:39.650
you don't do it well is just overhead for
doing it the times you do well.

00:37:39.650 --> 00:37:42.560
And this is probably the biggest
distinction that business people do

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:43.180
not understand.

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:46.100
because what they want is actually teeny,

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:50.210
little uninteresting projects that
are guaranteed for your success.

00:37:52.410 --> 00:37:53.990
But sports is right in front of it.

00:37:53.990 --> 00:38:01.410
Sports is really hard, and most sports
people are not succeeding all the time.

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:02.760
Anybody know baseball here?

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:04.493
What's the thing in baseball,
it's called an error?

00:38:06.875 --> 00:38:08.505
Like what?

00:38:08.505 --> 00:38:12.420
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; No, just fielding.

00:38:12.420 --> 00:38:12.920
&gt;&gt; Error.

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:14.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah, what's an error in baseball?

00:38:14.170 --> 00:38:17.120
Not catching a fly ball.

00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:20.708
How good are the average fielders?

00:38:20.708 --> 00:38:26.020
They're 98.5% effective.

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:30.060
So, really good ones are like one
percent error and so an error and

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:36.010
technical stuff is designed to build
a computer system and failing to build it.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:40.990
Like anybody should be, decide to build
a software and failing to build it.

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:42.351
You just should be able to knock that off.

00:38:42.351 --> 00:38:46.150
But you're in this other range
when you're trying to do design.

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:48.988
And it's really two different things.

00:38:48.988 --> 00:38:52.626
So, here's a memo Lick wrote in 63,

00:38:52.626 --> 00:38:58.474
shortly after he got this initial
money from ARPA to members and

00:38:58.474 --> 00:39:03.570
affiliates of
the Intergalactic Computing Network.

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:06.799
And they asked him,
why did you call it that?

00:39:06.799 --> 00:39:11.330
He said, well engineers always give you
the minimum, and I want a network that

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:15.480
spans the entire planet, so
I'm asking for an intergalactic one.

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:18.237
And when they scale it down,
we'll still get, so

00:39:18.237 --> 00:39:21.138
that's where the internet came from,
literally.

00:39:21.138 --> 00:39:24.133
The original name of the Internet
was the intergalactic computing,

00:39:24.133 --> 00:39:25.640
nobody knew how to do it back then.

00:39:26.910 --> 00:39:31.296
For instance, packet switching
had not been invented in 63.

00:39:31.296 --> 00:39:35.640
And here's a nice line in that memo,
if we succeed in making an intergalactic

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:38.640
network, then our our main problem will
be learning to communicate with aliens.

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:41.620
And he meant this in
the biggest possible way, and

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:44.620
I don't have time to really explain it,
it's really interesting.

00:39:45.620 --> 00:39:47.853
People who are interested in
this should write me an email.

00:39:50.063 --> 00:39:51.046
He didn't mean just other people.

00:39:51.046 --> 00:39:54.471
He meant other software, other computers.

00:39:54.471 --> 00:39:57.670
He meant what does it mean to
communicate when you scale things up?

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:01.723
So this guy was a big thinker.

00:40:01.723 --> 00:40:03.910
Number nine,
you can't think inside the Beltway.

00:40:03.910 --> 00:40:05.940
That's of course referring to Washington,
DC.

00:40:05.940 --> 00:40:09.073
But this is a general principle.

00:40:09.073 --> 00:40:12.316
They asked him why, and he said,
well, because there's too much noise.

00:40:12.316 --> 00:40:13.440
There's just too much politics.

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:15.184
There's too much ****.

00:40:15.184 --> 00:40:17.450
And nobody does research in Washington,
DC.

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:22.241
So the last thing we want to do,
as our profounders, is to try and

00:40:22.241 --> 00:40:23.568
think while we're there.

00:40:23.568 --> 00:40:27.920
Our job is to get money out of
the government and pass it along.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:31.565
And so a solution to that would say,
hey let's not be here for

00:40:31.565 --> 00:40:33.008
longer than two years.

00:40:33.008 --> 00:40:37.849
And so every two years, so Lick set
up the process to get his successor,

00:40:37.849 --> 00:40:43.272
happened that Ivan Sutherland got drafted
into the army at just the right time.

00:40:43.272 --> 00:40:45.998
And so they grabbed him and so

00:40:45.998 --> 00:40:51.230
at age 26 Ivan ran this whole show and
man was he good.

00:40:52.715 --> 00:40:58.315
He was a second lieutenant sharing
meetings with generals and

00:40:58.315 --> 00:41:02.305
one of the most famous
things recalled of that.

00:41:02.305 --> 00:41:05.905
Some general was going on and
on at some point Ivan said,

00:41:05.905 --> 00:41:09.295
general you have just two minutes
to make your point if you have one.

00:41:11.235 --> 00:41:15.259
That's about the simplest
way of describing Ivan.

00:41:15.259 --> 00:41:19.390
Bob Taylor and Larry Roberts.

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:21.910
Those are the four that made this happen.

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:25.917
Taylor is special because he is
also the guy who set up park.

00:41:25.917 --> 00:41:27.530
And he was a student of Licklider.

00:41:27.530 --> 00:41:29.536
Licklider was intuitively wonderful,

00:41:29.536 --> 00:41:32.700
Taylor was a student of what
was wonderful about Licklider.

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:37.830
And he could explain everything that
Licklider could do and why it worked.

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:43.390
So here's a couple things,
I'm not going to go about, but

00:41:43.390 --> 00:41:47.725
basically the idea here is it
isn't like lets look around and

00:41:47.725 --> 00:41:51.132
see what is available and
what we can do with it.

00:41:51.132 --> 00:41:54.158
The idea was stick with the vision and

00:41:54.158 --> 00:41:58.460
just make every frigging
thing that's necessary.

00:41:58.460 --> 00:41:59.520
It doesn't matter what it is.

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:02.350
If we have to make a new kind of
integrated circuit, we'll do it.

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:04.510
All of these things were done.

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:10.691
So it's very much like these other
cultures where nobody worried about

00:42:10.691 --> 00:42:16.577
whether you had half centimeter radar in
building 20 at MIT, they just did it.

00:42:16.577 --> 00:42:22.039
And here's one that is really a bug today.

00:42:22.039 --> 00:42:24.826
And if you don't have the chops,
yeah you shouldn't make your tools,

00:42:24.826 --> 00:42:26.736
you shouldn't make your
own operating system.

00:42:26.736 --> 00:42:30.061
You shouldn't make your own programming
language because that's not what you're

00:42:30.061 --> 00:42:30.631
trying to do.

00:42:30.631 --> 00:42:34.562
If you do have the chops, and you better
not do his unless you have the chops,

00:42:34.562 --> 00:42:37.389
then you have to make your
own hardware and software and

00:42:37.389 --> 00:42:40.250
operating system in
the programming languages.

00:42:40.250 --> 00:42:44.770
Otherwise you're working in the past
on some vendor's bad idea of what

00:42:44.770 --> 00:42:45.510
computing is about.

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:51.380
So part of this deal,
remember what Picasso said.

00:42:51.380 --> 00:42:53.790
You have to get really much,
much better than

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:59.100
what most people want to today,
and here's another reversal.

00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:01.704
Today people buy hardware and

00:43:01.704 --> 00:43:07.026
put software on it basically to
make the hardware look good.

00:43:07.026 --> 00:43:10.100
The ARPA community in part
did exactly the opposite.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:11.837
It started off with the software.

00:43:11.837 --> 00:43:16.310
Using super computers
to simulate the thing.

00:43:16.310 --> 00:43:18.740
And when you got the software
the way you wanted it,

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:22.320
then you'd design the computer
that would optimize and

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:24.880
make more efficient what you
were doing on a super computer.

00:43:24.880 --> 00:43:26.140
That is what Sketchpad was.

00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:31.119
Nobody thought that the next graphic
systems were going to be done on

00:43:31.119 --> 00:43:33.409
a computer the size of this building.

00:43:35.779 --> 00:43:38.950
Okay, and then make a bunch of them.

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:41.221
So almost every project
in the ARPA project,

00:43:41.221 --> 00:43:44.460
they actually made enough of them so
they could be used as tools.

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.928
They weren't just demos.

00:43:46.928 --> 00:43:52.880
And so part of this invention process had
to require a kind of limited engineering.

00:43:52.880 --> 00:43:53.480
Park, for

00:43:53.480 --> 00:43:57.756
instance, had a thing where whatever
you did you had to make 100 of them.

00:43:57.756 --> 00:44:00.190
Made an Ethernet,
it had to run 100 machines.

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:03.070
If you made a time sharing system,
it had run 100 users.

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:05.567
If you made a personal computer,
you had to be able to build 100 of them.

00:44:08.609 --> 00:44:10.700
Learn how to argue.

00:44:10.700 --> 00:44:12.740
This is what you learned as
a graduate student there.

00:44:14.210 --> 00:44:16.310
Argue for clarity, not to win.

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:18.290
It's the biggest bug today.

00:44:18.290 --> 00:44:22.770
People are always contending with each
other, trying to be winners and losers,

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:25.200
and that's one of
the problems with Washington,

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:26.730
they've forgotten what their job is.

00:44:28.530 --> 00:44:31.240
Now what you have to do is
understand these complicated things.

00:44:32.600 --> 00:44:38.210
Here's a biggie,
every researcher at Park was a second or

00:44:38.210 --> 00:44:41.420
third generation PhD
that ARPA had created.

00:44:41.420 --> 00:44:45.389
Right, eight years.

00:44:45.389 --> 00:44:47.251
It wasn't just the stuff in the past.

00:44:47.251 --> 00:44:51.661
I'll show you a couple more of those,
wow we're getting closed to, but

00:44:51.661 --> 00:44:53.990
it was creating the next generation.

00:44:53.990 --> 00:44:54.900
Again, this is baseball.

00:44:55.900 --> 00:45:02.350
Baseball you have to develop talent going
all the way the down to little league.

00:45:02.350 --> 00:45:07.173
And they did and
the other thing is a little difficult to,

00:45:07.173 --> 00:45:10.801
some of you will understand this readily.

00:45:10.801 --> 00:45:15.636
That the reward of doing this stuff
wasn't the reward of making it happen

00:45:15.636 --> 00:45:18.100
because a lot of times it didn't happen.

00:45:18.100 --> 00:45:24.320
It was a reward of actually being funded
to work on what the actual problems were.

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:29.180
I can't emphasize this too much.

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:35.160
Working on what the problems actually are
rather than something that's going to get

00:45:35.160 --> 00:45:40.800
a paper, something that's going to make
you money, but taking big human problems.

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:43.278
Could be something like drinkable water.

00:45:43.278 --> 00:45:46.928
For the 70% of the planet
that doesn't have it.

00:45:46.928 --> 00:45:48.980
Doesn't matter, there's no other goal.

00:45:48.980 --> 00:45:54.509
If you pick one of those things that's
a great one, worth putting your life into.

00:45:54.509 --> 00:45:59.348
Okay, so background was partly tinkering.

00:46:01.312 --> 00:46:06.700
A lot of people came from New York,
down where the world trade center was.

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:11.363
Before the world trade center, there was
about a mile across Manhattan Island that

00:46:11.363 --> 00:46:13.903
was nothing but electronic surplus stores.

00:46:13.903 --> 00:46:18.284
Mow a few lawns,
put a dime into the subway and

00:46:18.284 --> 00:46:24.866
you could go down there and
buy almost anything to mess around with.

00:46:24.866 --> 00:46:31.060
And that group got used to,
everybody was broke.

00:46:31.060 --> 00:46:34.370
There was a kit to build
your own oscilloscope.

00:46:34.370 --> 00:46:37.730
And if you wanted an oscilloscope and
you were broke,

00:46:38.770 --> 00:46:43.200
you could get one of these on time for
$30 or $40 bucks and build it.

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:47.280
And I'm going to just

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:53.830
put, these are just washed out anyway so
I'm just going to go past.

00:46:57.260 --> 00:47:01.678
Okay, so Moore's Law, this was
the original thing in Moore's paper.

00:47:01.678 --> 00:47:06.592
Doubling every year.

00:47:06.592 --> 00:47:10.141
He picked MOS silicon which is too
slow to make things out of, but

00:47:10.141 --> 00:47:12.390
would get faster if you made it smaller.

00:47:12.390 --> 00:47:16.945
Here's doubling every two years,
and what happened.

00:47:16.945 --> 00:47:21.945
The prediction was 30 years and so
what happened was, very in line

00:47:21.945 --> 00:47:28.368
with doubling every two years to every 18
months, and there is physics behind this.

00:47:28.368 --> 00:47:31.992
This wasn't just an engineering
aspiration, and so

00:47:31.992 --> 00:47:35.947
if you believe this you had
something really worthwhile.

00:47:35.947 --> 00:47:36.717
And I'm going to skip past Engelbart here.

